From 4f49640f5605fa7cf008d73f20069a2bbabc3277 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:47:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades] HDMI FRL kept only from 296 MHz, and with VRR, Adjust
 display resolution wins

HDMI FRL kept only from 296 MHz. An LG C5 (RTX 4080 SUPER, menus
at 3840x2160 and 120 Hz, which need FRL) stayed black
when a 1080p film started with "Adjust display resolution": 1920x1080 at
23.977 Hz, a pixel clock of 74 MHz, kept over FRL at 4 lanes of 8 Gb/s
(NV_HDMI_FRL_KEEP, FrlKeep.h). NVKMS and the display engine finished that mode
set (the raster runs), the link trained at the first try, the display said over
SCDC that its four lanes were locked, the flips went on, and Kodi's hot-plug
handling did nothing (HOTPLUG_RELINK: SINK_KEPT, as for the same mode a few minutes
later, which worked). The only differences from that one: that mode set also took HDMI
VRR from on to off (the VRR setting had been turned off in the menus, which
waited for the next mode set, and a VTEM with VRR off went out with it), and
the display's SCDC had valid error counts on all four lanes, 546 Reed-Solomon
corrections. A picture lost that way cannot be seen from the source, so nothing
can bring it back. The 33 modes of 296.7 and 297 MHz kept over FRL in the logs
of the first builds of 1.1 all went; of the two of 74 MHz, one did.

- FRL_KEEP::ForMode(), MIN_KEPT_KHZ (296 MHz): NV_HDMI_FRL_KEEP stays 0 in the
  mode set of a mode under it, which goes over TMDS as NVKMS chooses; modes of
  296 MHz and more (3840x2160 at 23.976 to 30 Hz, 1920x1080 at 100 and 120 Hz)
  stay on FRL as before. The way back to the menus over FRL turns the output
  off first (FRL_MODE_CHANGE::OffFirst()): all 18 such changes in the logs of
  the first builds of 1.1 went (6 on the LG C5, 12 on a Samsung Odyssey G60SD), where 4 of
  35 made without it hung (driver 620.70.06). Where one hangs, its flips stop,
  which Kodi sees, and the menus go to a TMDS mode of their size until the next
  video (FRL_MODE_CHANGE::Fallback()). So a 1080p film goes out at 1920x1080 at
  its own rate. kodi.log: "HDMI FRL: ... is not kept on FRL".
- The change of the VRR setting in the menus goes out at once since the commit
  before this one, so the mode set of a video no longer takes HDMI VRR from on
  to off where the setting had been turned off.

With VRR, Adjust display resolution wins. With "Video at its own rate with VRR"
on, the setting took the video in the mode of the menus before "Adjust display
resolution" was asked, so the resolution never changed (an LG C5 and a
Samsung Odyssey G60SD). Now:

- VIDEO_SIZE::ChooseForVrr() before the two places where VRR may take the
  video: of the modes of the video's size that carry it with VRR on this output
  (VrrModeOk(): the display's VRR range; NVKMS's 50 Hz, 720 lines and 110 % of
  its minimum; over FRL kept for the menus, none under MIN_KEPT_KHZ, which would
  go over TMDS; over TMDS, none over 600 MHz: a video at its own rate never
  changes the link), the nearest in size and then the one of the highest rate:
  1920x1080 at 120 Hz on the LG C5, 1920x1080 at 359.986 Hz on the G60SD whose
  menus go over FRL. TakeVideoAtOwnRate() takes it as the mode of the video
  (sized): one change of mode, whose mode set allows HDMI VRR, and the check of
  the first second as always.
- Where the display has modes of that size and none with VRR, VRR is not asked
  (ForgetVideoAtOwnRate()): Kodi's change of mode to the exact rate, as without
  VRR; where no mode of that size is at that rate either, the video goes at its
  own rate in the mode of the menus, as before. Without a mode of that size at
  all (a 4K film on a monitor of 2560x1440), the menus, as before.
- CWinSystemBase::ResolutionClockKHz() and VideoModeLink(): the pixel clock of
  the mode of a resolution, and what the output allows of one for a video.

The Samsung Odyssey G60SD with VRR off and its menus at 2560x1440 at 359.999 Hz:
its modes of 1920x1080 are at 359.986, 239.927, 120, 119.880, 60 and 59.940 Hz;
none is within Kodi's 0.01 Hz of the 359.999 Hz that Kodi keeps for a film
there (it has no mode at the rate of films), so the size stays, as before.

The help of "Adjust display resolution", "Video at its own rate with VRR" and
"HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)" says it (core #62141, #62143, #62146, Estuary
#54141, #54143, #54146; en_gb, es_es, es_ar, es_mx).
