 xbmc/windowing/VideoSizeMode.h                | 109 +++++++++++++-
 xbmc/windowing/WinSystem.h                    |  19 ++-
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/FrlKeep.h                  |  25 ++++
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbm.cpp           | 136 ++++++++++++++++--
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbm.h             |   9 +-
 11 files changed, 421 insertions(+), 56 deletions(-)

diff --git a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
index 09dd952..ef8eb1d 100644
--- a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
+++ b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
@@ -27904,7 +27904,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadeshdmivrr"; translated in Estuary (#54141)
 msgctxt "#62141"
-msgid "Kodi does not change the refresh rate from one frame to the next, except for \"Video at its own rate with VRR\", so otherwise it has no use for VRR (G-SYNC, FreeSync), and some TVs go to their game mode while VRR is on. NVIDIA graphics tell an HDMI TV that declares VRR that it is on, in the modes from 50 Hz up that its VRR range covers, unless Kodi asks them not to. Off (the default): Kodi asks the driver to leave VRR off in every change of mode, so the TV is told that it is off; the picture Kodi sends does not change. On: the driver decides. \"Video at its own rate with VRR\" leaves it to the driver in every change of mode while it is at Always, and at \"Where the display has no mode at the video's rate\" in the modes of the menus of a display that declares VRR and has no mode at the rate of films (23.976 Hz, or twice it) at any resolution, so that a video can play at its own rate there. Dolby Vision always goes out with VRR off. Applies from the next change of mode, for example when the next video starts and changes the refresh rate."
+msgid "Kodi does not change the refresh rate from one frame to the next, except for \"Video at its own rate with VRR\", so otherwise it has no use for VRR (G-SYNC, FreeSync), and some TVs go to their game mode while VRR is on. NVIDIA graphics tell an HDMI TV that declares VRR that it is on, in the modes from 50 Hz up that its VRR range covers, unless Kodi asks them not to. Off (the default): Kodi asks the driver to leave VRR off in every change of mode, so the TV is told that it is off; the picture Kodi sends does not change. On: the driver decides. \"Video at its own rate with VRR\" leaves it to the driver in every change of mode while it is at Always, and at \"Where the display has no mode at the video's rate\" in the modes of the menus of a display that declares VRR and has no mode at the rate of films (23.976 Hz, or twice it) at any resolution, so that a video can play at its own rate there. Dolby Vision always goes out with VRR off. Applies at once: the mode of the menus is set again (the screen goes black for a moment); during a video, from the change of mode at its end."
 msgstr ""
 
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
@@ -27916,7 +27916,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesvrrvideomode"; translated in Estuary (#54143)
 msgctxt "#62143"
-msgid "Plays a video at its own frame rate with the display's variable refresh rate (VRR), in the mode of the menus: each picture is shown at its own time, and where the frame rate is under the lowest rate of the display's VRR, each picture is shown two or more times. Off (the default): videos play as always, with a change of mode to their rate where the display has one. Where the display has no mode at the video's rate: only where the display has no mode at the frame rate of a video, or at twice it, at any resolution (a monitor without modes of 24 Hz, for example), instead of a fixed refresh that shows some pictures one refresh longer than others; a display with such a mode (a TV with modes of 24 Hz) changes to it as always. Always: on any display with VRR, also where it has a mode at the video's rate, so the screen does not go black when a video starts or stops. SDR and HDR10 videos and, where the graphics driver sends HDR10+ next to VRR (NVIDIA RTX 40 and RTX 50 series), HDR10+ and Dolby Vision that goes out as HDR10, HDR10+ or SDR (on a display without Dolby Vision). Dolby Vision sent as Dolby Vision, HLG and 3D change the mode to the video's rate as always: Dolby Vision needs modes of its own of up to 60 Hz, and no display says that it takes it with VRR. Needs \"Adjust display refresh rate\", and VRR turned on in the display itself (Adaptive-Sync or FreeSync on a monitor; on an LG TV, VRR & G-Sync for NVIDIA graphics and AMD FreeSync Premium for AMD graphics): the display does not say whether it is on. On NVIDIA it leaves \"HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)\" to the driver in the changes of mode that need it. In the first second of each video LibreELEC Hades checks with the graphics driver, and on NVIDIA also by when each picture reached the output, that the refresh of the output follows the video; where it does not, the video plays as with this setting off, and the player information says so (System page, VRR). Below the lowest rate of the display's VRR a video cannot go at its own rate: 23.976 fps goes at 47.952 Hz (each picture twice) to a TV whose VRR starts at 40 Hz, and at 71.928 Hz (three times) to a monitor whose VRR starts at 48 Hz, with the same even motion. With VRR on, the display shows that rate in its own information (47 or 72 Hz, for example), and so does the player information, measured (System page). kodi.log says what was chosen for each video."
+msgid "Plays a video at its own frame rate with the display's variable refresh rate (VRR), in the mode of the menus (with \"Adjust display resolution\" on, in the mode of its own size: see that setting): each picture is shown at its own time, and where the frame rate is under the lowest rate of the display's VRR, each picture is shown two or more times. Off (the default): videos play as always, with a change of mode to their rate where the display has one. Where the display has no mode at the video's rate: only where the display has no mode at the frame rate of a video, or at twice it, at any resolution (a monitor without modes of 24 Hz, for example), instead of a fixed refresh that shows some pictures one refresh longer than others; a display with such a mode (a TV with modes of 24 Hz) changes to it as always. Always: on any display with VRR, also where it has a mode at the video's rate, so the screen does not go black when a video starts or stops. SDR and HDR10 videos and, where the graphics driver sends HDR10+ next to VRR (NVIDIA RTX 40 and RTX 50 series), HDR10+ and Dolby Vision that goes out as HDR10, HDR10+ or SDR (on a display without Dolby Vision). Dolby Vision sent as Dolby Vision, HLG and 3D change the mode to the video's rate as always: Dolby Vision needs modes of its own of up to 60 Hz, and no display says that it takes it with VRR. Needs \"Adjust display refresh rate\", and VRR turned on in the display itself (Adaptive-Sync or FreeSync on a monitor; on an LG TV, VRR & G-Sync for NVIDIA graphics and AMD FreeSync Premium for AMD graphics): the display does not say whether it is on. On NVIDIA it leaves \"HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)\" to the driver in the changes of mode that need it; where a change of this setting changes that, the mode of the menus is set again at once (the screen goes black for a moment), so that the next video finds it. In the first second of each video LibreELEC Hades checks with the graphics driver, and on NVIDIA also by when each picture reached the output, that the refresh of the output follows the video; where it does not, the video plays as with this setting off, and the player information says so (System page, VRR). Below the lowest rate of the display's VRR a video cannot go at its own rate: 23.976 fps goes at 47.952 Hz (each picture twice) to a TV whose VRR starts at 40 Hz, and at 71.928 Hz (three times) to a monitor whose VRR starts at 48 Hz, with the same even motion. With VRR on, the display shows that rate in its own information (47 or 72 Hz, for example), and so does the player information, measured (System page). kodi.log says what was chosen for each video."
 msgstr ""
 
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
@@ -27934,7 +27934,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoplayer.hadesadjustresolution"; translated in Estuary (#54146)
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#62146"
-msgid "Plays a video in a mode of the display of its own size, at the refresh rate that \"Adjust display refresh rate\" chooses for it: a film of 1920x1080 goes out at 1920x1080 on a TV whose menus are at 3840x2160, and the TV scales it. Off (the default): the video goes out at the size Kodi chooses, usually that of the menus, and the graphics card scales it; the upscalers of LibreELEC Hades (the Video page of the player information) and Kodi's scaling method only change the picture where the graphics card scales it. Where the display has no mode of the video's size at that refresh rate, the video plays as with this setting off. Native Dolby Vision (sent to a TV that has Dolby Vision) always goes out at 3840x2160: LibreELEC Hades makes its Dolby Vision signal at that size only, so the graphics card enlarges a 1080p Dolby Vision video. With a whitelist (System > Display > Whitelist) the whitelist chooses the modes and their sizes, and this setting does nothing. Where \"Video at its own rate with VRR\" takes a video, it plays in the mode of the menus. Needs \"Adjust display refresh rate\". kodi.log says what was chosen for each video."
+msgid "Plays a video in a mode of the display of its own size, at the refresh rate that \"Adjust display refresh rate\" chooses for it: a film of 1920x1080 goes out at 1920x1080 on a TV whose menus are at 3840x2160, and the TV scales it. Off (the default): the video goes out at the size Kodi chooses, usually that of the menus, and the graphics card scales it; the upscalers of LibreELEC Hades (the Video page of the player information) and Kodi's scaling method only change the picture where the graphics card scales it. Where the display has no mode of the video's size at that refresh rate, the video plays as with this setting off. Where the menus need the fastest link of HDMI 2.1 (FRL: 3840x2160 at 120 Hz on NVIDIA graphics), a video mode of less than 296 MHz (1920x1080 at 23.976 to 60 Hz) goes over the older TMDS link, as NVIDIA's driver chooses it; the way back to the menus turns the output off first, and where the picture does not come back the menus go to 3840x2160 at 60 Hz until the next video. Native Dolby Vision (sent to a TV that has Dolby Vision) always goes out at 3840x2160: LibreELEC Hades makes its Dolby Vision signal at that size only, so the graphics card enlarges a 1080p Dolby Vision video. With a whitelist (System > Display > Whitelist) the whitelist chooses the modes and their sizes, and this setting does nothing. With \"Video at its own rate with VRR\" on as well, the size wins: the video goes in one change of mode to the mode of its size with the highest refresh rate in the display's VRR range (1920x1080 at 120 Hz on an LG C5), at its own rate with VRR in it; where the display has modes of that size and none with VRR, the change of mode is to the exact rate, as without VRR. Needs \"Adjust display refresh rate\". kodi.log says what was chosen for each video."
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: label of setting "audiooutput.hadespconaudio"; translated in Estuary (#52978)
